<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bonaire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bonaire is an island in the Leeward Antilles of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, east of Curaçao and off the coast of Venezuela. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> claimed it from 1499, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> took it in 1636, using it for salt production and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raising worked by enslaved Africans. It was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> Antilles until 2010, when it became a special municipality of the Netherlands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bonaire.jpg" alt="Bonaire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike Rohsopht · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bonaire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bonaire is one of the ABC islands, with Aruba and Curaçao, about 80 kilometres off the coast of Venezuela. It is 38.6 kilometres long from north to south and 5 to 8 kilometres wide, with a land area of 288 square kilometres. Geologists believe the ABC islands were formed about 90 million years ago along the Leeward Antilles Ridge, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> plate collided with the South American plate. The climate is warm, dry and windy, which favours cacti and other desert plants. The islet of Klein Bonaire lies 800 metres to the west, and the coast is fringed by a coral reef.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest evidence of human habitation on Bonaire is at Lagun and dates to about 3400 years ago, when people lived as semi-nomadic hunter-gatherers and fishers. The Caquetío, a people from Venezuela, arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> around 500 CE, and their rock paintings and petroglyphs survive in caves at Spelonk, Onima, Ceru Pungi and Ceru Crita-Cabai. Alonso de Ojeda reached the area in 1499, accompanied by Amerigo Vespucci and Juan de la Cosa. Because the islands had no known metal deposits the Spanish thought them useless, and in 1515 they enslaved the Caquetío to work in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, perhaps between 500 and 2,000 people.</p><p>From 1623 ships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> called at Bonaire for meat, water and wood. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, having lost Sint Maarten to the Spanish during the Eighty Years War, attacked Curaçao, Bonaire and Aruba, and conquered Bonaire in March 1636. They built Fort Oranje in 1639. In 1868 and 1870 much government land was auctioned, mostly to wealthy merchants of Curaçao who did not live there; the soil is poor for crops, and only salt extraction proved economically attractive, with aloe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> and goats on some holdings. On 10 October 2010 the Netherlands Antilles was dissolved and Bonaire became a special municipality of the Netherlands, a public body under article 134 of the Dutch Constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bonaire's seat is Kralendijk, and Rincon is the oldest settlement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> is the official language, but most inhabitants use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papiamento">Papiamentu</a> as their main language, and about 68 percent are Roman Catholic. The island has been a nature reserve for decades: Washington Slagbaai National Park was established in 1969, and the Bonaire National Marine Park, covering the whole coastline, in 1979. Several wetlands are recognised under the Ramsar Convention, and the Pekelmeer flamingo reserve is the most important breeding site in the Southern Caribbean. Salt pans cover about 10 percent of the island, and salt is still produced there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bonaire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since 2010 the Kingdom of the Netherlands has consisted of four countries, the Netherlands, Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten, while <b>Bonaire</b>, Sint Eustatius and Saba are special Dutch municipalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Aruba and <b>Bonaire</b> were taken soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-curacao">Colony of Curaçao</a></li><li><blockquote>It comprised Curaçao, Aruba and <b>Bonaire</b> off Venezuela, and Sint Maarten, Saba and Sint Eustatius in the Leewards.</blockquote><a href="/state/netherlands-antilles">Netherlands Antilles</a></li></ul></section>
